#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Step Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f731d1-9141-5228-85f8-725a2f8d01db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220650} {:answer/id #uuid "924bb152-41f8-528d-aeda-f3f74429aeb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220651} {:answer/id #uuid "d60df248-42c7-5bd7-b123-e97428e887d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220652} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0bfffc-9493-57b7-b796-721d7239fa9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "924bb152-41f8-528d-aeda-f3f74429aeb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220651} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03eb30c-b9f8-585b-902a-e9885064323a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5b53b94-7a02-5a61-97c8-d048e12128ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-339627" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+({x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1+1=2.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =1+3\\cdot (-1)+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈0.00.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Step Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6104dece-ab43-592e-b362-fee520aa6d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339876} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5429d4-76ef-51c5-af6d-776ad7c40146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339877} {:answer/id #uuid "89bc3d7c-16c2-555b-b82a-cade8738d470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339878} {:answer/id #uuid "3840961d-6d6c-50ac-ac0d-17593dc3540b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e5429d4-76ef-51c5-af6d-776ad7c40146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339877} :answer-field/id #uuid "200ca093-e0fa-528a-bba6-c1a1e92ee49c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f65d0be-ba21-5548-be1c-19a3b018adff" :question/math-academy-id "q-339749" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5x+3y,\\,y(-1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-(-1)=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5x+3y+(5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+3{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+3{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y+3{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-1+2=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(5\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈2.00.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Step Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37fa1512-feac-54c2-80b4-ab3d44036ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339882} {:answer/id #uuid "f218bcec-74b2-5179-a4ce-604e78c65bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339883} {:answer/id #uuid "e524c457-72a2-531a-98c7-2f593c6503c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339884} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc48b38-068e-5656-839b-d35c2e1dd67b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e524c457-72a2-531a-98c7-2f593c6503c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339884} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca8bea33-3a84-5b7a-9945-9171d82cb0fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d767b77-2ccf-57b2-a4a4-c4c6157cb523" :question/math-academy-id "q-339756" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-1}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2y+({x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈1.00.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Steps Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4c8f3de-6d88-5cf7-bc9c-807f87f5b175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 1.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220694} {:answer/id #uuid "ca934514-cf6c-592f-a3e0-8a7e9a8043a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220695} {:answer/id #uuid "5d776e7f-3266-5819-a70a-78ea8c12c2d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca934514-cf6c-592f-a3e0-8a7e9a8043a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220695} :answer-field/id #uuid "45491dea-33b8-50ff-af4b-b0e11707702f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220693} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "274898e0-a831-5f5b-a529-3e6b83da0d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220698} {:answer/id #uuid "42381fda-6bda-531a-93cf-044471555f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 1.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220699} {:answer/id #uuid "5b63cfa0-ebc7-5924-9c14-a1ddc314208d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "274898e0-a831-5f5b-a529-3e6b83da0d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220698} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b37c8a4-15cd-5737-85cc-363db702569d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f888fbe-20c0-5862-9487-c8905d7f153c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339772" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x-3y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{7} \\\\  & ≈1.14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{3}{2})≈1.00,\\,y(2)≈1.14.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Steps Using the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9a4e90f-1d61-53b8-81b4-a2b28e2280f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339888} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7a4932-8500-552f-bd90-b5638851a8dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339889} {:answer/id #uuid "27ad54fb-9ca4-545c-be65-41f9016c4e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27ad54fb-9ca4-545c-be65-41f9016c4e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339890} :answer-field/id #uuid "c32946d0-5ac7-51fa-9ac4-a76e6873fbd3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339887} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ce459e-5938-54fd-b9e0-f140f6edf511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339892} {:answer/id #uuid "fab4fb6d-fe99-576f-9af2-2b8707984815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339893} {:answer/id #uuid "8aaa417c-8863-5b72-ad6e-bd4e068bb186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20ce459e-5938-54fd-b9e0-f140f6edf511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339892} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0544d37-814e-5cac-91a5-652907f5b7b2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8988821-0fed-5205-b021-83682db1c3eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-339871" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=25x+5y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{5})$ and $y\\,(\\frac{2}{5}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈{{field-1}},\\,y\\,(\\frac{2}{5})≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,(\\frac{2}{5}).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ is $\\frac{2}{5},$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\frac{2}{5}-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((25x+5y)+(25{x}_{\\text{new}}+5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{5})$ and $y(\\frac{2}{5}).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}+3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{5},\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}+5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{5})≈1.00,\\,y(\\frac{2}{5})≈6.00.\n$$"}]}
